// @run
// @log true
// @log false
// @log true
// @log false
// @log false
// @log true
// D989 — `==` between two VALUES of a scalar-carrying union. `vl check` returned 0 and emit
// refused with "union `==` atom has no value box".
//
// THE MESSAGE NAMED A SYMPTOM TWO LEVELS BELOW THE CAUSE, and the intermediate fix is a
// SILENT WRONG VALUE, so the acceptance condition here is this value table and not a compile.
//
// `emitUnionUnionEq` already implements tag-compare-then-per-arm. Its arm list is built by
// intersecting the two sides' member atoms, and for a declared union BOTH lookups miss:
// `msMemberAtomsOfSet` returns false and the fallbacks (`splitUnionAtoms` on the left,
// `unionHasAtom` on the right) answer for the union's own NAME. Expanding only the LEFT makes
// the program compile and `1 == 2` print `true` — the intersection is then empty, the arm
// loop emits nothing, and control reaches the tag-only fallback after it, which compares just
// the two TAGS. Both `1` and `2` are the i32 arm, so they "match".
//
// Expanding BOTH through `unionSetArmTys` gives the loop real arms and the payload compare
// runs. The value box was never the problem: `vbI32Used` forced true changes neither the
// refusal nor the wrong value.
//
// Union-against-LITERAL always ran — that is D972 — so this is its union-vs-union residue.
type U = i32 | string

const a: U = 1

const b: U = 1

const c: U = 2

const s1: U = "ab"

const s2: U = "ab"

const s3: U = "zz"

print(a == b)

print(a == c)

print(s1 == s2)

print(s1 == s3)

print(a == s1)

print(a != c)
